{"t":1791357210,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"d179eba7-e08b-4210-87d9-2c716ea7951e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-07T05:27:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 070527\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n127 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo major changes to previous forecast. Quiescent weather \nthrough Friday, rain possible this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Quiescent weather expected through the end of the week.\n\n- 2) Increasing rain potential Saturday night through Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of \nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore today turning the winds out of the\nsouth. This will yield a milder day with temps climbing back \ninto the 70s. Temps warm up to near 80 Thu before the passage \nof another dry cold front. Temps cool off slightly for the end \nof the week as weak high pressure of Canadian origin builds in. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increased rain potential late this weekend into\nearly next week. \n\nThe main feature to watch in the extended portion of the forecast \nwill be what becomes of Tropical Depression 9. Key steering features \nwill be a ridge in the western Atlantic, a trough/closed low over \nTexas, and eventually a trough ejecting from the northern Rockies. \nThe NHC forecast has the remnants of this system moving into the \nTennessee and Ohio Valleys over the weekend, although there still \nremains considerable uncertainty on the track and especially forward \nspeed of the system. Even despite this westward position of the \ncyclone, moisture from both the Gulf of America and Atlantic \nwill likely be transported northward into the Mid Atlantic. Rain\nwill become increasingly likely Sun, although as mentioned, \nthis timing could vary a bit. The remnant low could then move \neastward, potentially extending rain chances into Monday. At \nthis juncture, the rain will be the main impact from this \nsystem, but we will continue to monitor any changing trends.\n\nIt will be important to stay tuned to the forecast over the next few \ndays, and monitor hurricanes.gov for updated details on Tropical \nDepression 9.\n\nDaytime temperatures will likely be seasonal through the weekend and \nearly next week but will take a dip when rain is the most \nwidespread. Meanwhile overnight lows will be mild thanks to \nincreasing clouds and dew points.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through the end of the week. Winds\nturn S'ly today as high pressure shifts offshore. A dry cold \nfront Thu will cause another wind shift to the west and \nNW into Fri.\n\nVFR conditions with light south to southeast winds are expected \nSaturday. Rain and low ceilings are possible late Saturday night \ninto Sunday as tropical moisture is drawn northward into the\narea from the Gulf of America.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nWinds are expected to strengthen today out of the south and \ncould flirt with marginal SCA conditions this evening. Currently\nfavoring an MWS given the short duration and marginal nature. A\ndry frontal passage Thu could bring another period of marginal \nSCA conditions Thu afternoon into Thu night.\n\nWinds will become southeasterly Sat into Sat night, likely \nremaining below SCA levels. Winds could increase on Sun, \ndepending on the evolution of the weather pattern ahead of a \nremnant tropical system (current Tropical Depression 9) which \nwill likely be moving into the Tennessee or Ohio Valleys.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/CPB\nAVIATION...ADS/CPB\nMARINE...ADS/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791357175,"days":{"2026-10-07":{"hi":72,"lo":54,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":78,"lo":52,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"